During gas conditioning before methanation, the water‑gas shift (WGS) step is used to <cite id="afa63324-8">turn the ratio between hydrogen and carbon monoxide in the synthesis gas</cite> by converting CO with steam into extra hydrogen: <cite id="6a93e68e-3">CO + H2O ⇌ CO2 + H2</cite>.  
So, to raise the H2/CO ratio, part of the CO in raw syngas is deliberately shifted to H2 and CO2 <cite id="afa63324-0">with the aid of a catalyst</cite>.

It is enhanced mainly by:
- <cite id="57ee5e88-7">higher steam content</cite>, because the WGS reaction is <cite id="57ee5e88-7">favored at lower temperatures and higher steam content</cite>;
- catalytic reactors, commonly using <cite id="57ee5e88-0">Cu-Zn and Fe-Cr catalysts</cite>;
- temperature staging, since WGS is often run in <cite id="6a93e68e-2">two stages: a high temperature stage</cite> followed by cooling and a low‑temperature stage, where <cite id="6a93e68e-8">to achieve higher conversions of CO to H2, the gas leaving the HTS reactor is cooled to 200-220°C and passed through a low-temperature shift reactor</cite>;
- sulfur-tolerant catalysts where needed, because <cite id="6a93e68e-1">a novel sulfur tolerant water gas shift (SWGS) catalyst has been developed</cite> for operation under challenging gas conditions.

An example of the effect is that <cite id="6a93e68e-5">the H2/CO ratio increased to 2.36 and the CO conversion increased to 51.70% when the steam/CO ratio was 3.44</cite>.

In short: WGS conditioning boosts methanation feed quality by reacting syngas CO with added steam over shift catalysts, usually in staged high‑ and low‑temperature reactors, to convert CO into H2 and thereby increase the H2/CO ratio while producing removable CO2.
